{"_meta":{"backend":"deepseek","recorded":"live","date":"2026-10-13","agent_version":"0.0.1","agent_name":"deepseek-harness-acp","note":"Captured off the wire from `dsh --profile acp` (package @deepseek-ai/dsh 0.2.0-rc.2; the bridge reports agentInfo.version 0.0.1) with Kiro Crew's gate plugin (src/kiro_crew/agent_sdk/gate_extensions/deepseek/kiro_crew_tool_gate.mjs) composed through a `--patch` file carrying the same four rows the client writes, under DSH_PERMISSION_MODE=workspace-write. This is the evidence the deepseek member of ACP_BACKENDS_SIDE_READONLY rests on. Six calls in one turn, one per model response: the harness's own read, grep and glob, its write and bash, and a call to a tool the harness does not have, named `mcp__probe__read`. Every one raised session/request_permission, and every tool_call frame states the tool's own name as its `title` with `kind: other` and no `_meta`; the permission frame carries only the toolCallId. The client answered allow-once for read, grep and glob, which completed, and reject-once for the other three, which failed with `the user rejected tool`; the write and the bash left no file in the work dir. Driven by a model served locally on loopback (an Anthropic Messages responder scripted to emit one tool_use block per response), so no provider credential was involved. Agent-to-client lines are verbatim except the session id, which is replaced with a fixed synthetic value, and the work dir, which is replaced with /work in the read result."}}
